(1. MORPHOLOGICAL TYPOLOGY & ROOT CLASSES) Tj
0 -16 Td
/F1 8 Tf
( ) Tj
0 -12 Td
/F1 9 Tf
(Tangkhul word formation operates primarily through agglutination with) Tj
0 -13 Td
/F1 9 Tf
(strict suffixal dominance in nominal inflection and prefixal dominance) Tj
0 -13 Td
/F1 9 Tf
(in verbal derivation. Monosyllabic lexical roots fall into three major) Tj
0 -13 Td
/F1 9 Tf
(syntactic categories: Noun roots \(shim 'house', ram 'country'\), Verb) Tj
0 -13 Td
/F1 9 Tf
(roots \(sa 'do', thei 'see', zat 'go'\), and Deictic/Particle bases \(hi) Tj
0 -13 Td
/F1 9 Tf
('this', chi 'that'\). Nominal roots can occur bare as free morphemes,) Tj
0 -13 Td
/F1 9 Tf
(whereas verbal roots generally require affixes or compounding.) Tj
0 -13 Td
/F1 8 Tf
( ) Tj
0 -12 Td
/F2 10 Tf
(Tangkhul Linguistic Data & Analysis:) Tj
0 -14 Td
/F2 9 Tf
(  * shim \(house\) -> shimkhar \(village community\) -> shimsak \(construction\) : Nominal root extension) Tj
0 -13 Td
/F1 8 Tf
(    Analysis: Root compounding.) Tj
0 -12 Td
/F2 9 Tf
(  * tui \(word\) -> tuitam \(lesson\) -> tuingashik \(covenant\) : Discourse compounding) Tj
0 -13 Td
/F1 8 Tf
(    Analysis: Semantic broadening.) Tj
0 -12 Td
